<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!--
      The HUD zone bar's height. Declared here and nowhere else on purpose: hud_field.xml sizes
      the ImageView from it and HudField reads the same resource both to size the bitmap it draws
      into and to tell the renderer how much of the tile the bar covers. A `26f * density` copy alongside the
      literal in the layout would not merely be a duplicate: toInt() truncates where Android's
      own dp conversion rounds (dp * density + 0.5f), so at a non-integer density the bitmap would
      come out a pixel shorter than the view fitXY stretches it into, and the row padding a pixel
      short of the bar. resources.getDimensionPixelSize applies the same rounding as the layout
      inflater, so the two cannot disagree.

      26dp because that is what the header row it replaces measures: FieldRenderer draws the
      header icon at 11.07dp * 1.4 with 5dp of padding on each side, which lands at 25.5dp.
    -->
    <dimen name="hud_bar_height">26dp</dimen>

    <!--
      Breathing room around a field's content: the number, the header, the zone bar's icon and
      value, and the inset the HUD's divider stops at. A resource and not a Kotlin constant
      because BOTH sides need it, and while it lived only in FieldRenderer the layout repeated it
      as a literal 5dp with a comment promising the two matched.

      Read with getDimensionPixelSize, which rounds the way the layout inflater does. The Kotlin
      constant it replaces multiplied by density and truncated, so at a non integer density the
      padding computed in code could land a pixel off the margin declared here.
    -->
    <dimen name="field_edge_padding">5dp</dimen>

    <!--
      How far the zone pill is inset from the top and bottom of the header row it sits in, so it
      reads as a lozenge laid in that row rather than as a block filling it.

      In px and not dp, for the same reason hud_divider_width is: this is a hairline of breathing
      room, and it is one pixel on every screen rather than something that thickens with density.

      BOTH sides need it, which is why it is a resource: hud_field.xml uses it as the pill's top
      margin, and HudField subtracts TWICE it from the header height to size the bitmap. A literal
      in one place and a constant in the other is how the gap at the bottom ends up different from
      the gap at the top.
    -->
    <dimen name="hud_pill_inset">1px</dimen>

    <!--
      The hairline between the HUD's two halves, in px rather than dp on purpose: it is a rule,
      and a rule is one pixel on every screen rather than something that thickens with density.

      Here rather than in the layout alone because HudField subtracts it before dividing the tile
      between the two slots. If the layout said 1px and the code assumed something else, every
      number in the tile would be scaled against a budget that does not match the space it is
      drawn into.
    -->
    <dimen name="hud_divider_width">1px</dimen>

</resources>
